Consumption (TSFC)*.Step 1: Determine Thrust Required for Cruise

For a 20 kg aircraft in steady, level cruise, the engine does not need to
produce 20 kg of thrust (approx. 196 N). Instead, thrust must equal
aerodynamic drag:
$$\text{Thrust Required } (T) =3D \frac{\text{Total
Weight}}{\text{Lift-to-Drag Ratio } (L/D)}$$

   -

   *Typical Turbojet UAV $L/D$:* ~6 to 10.
   -

   *Calculation (assuming $L/D =3D 8$):*
   $$T =3D \frac{20\text{ kg}}{8} =3D 2.5\text{ kg of thrust} \approx
   24.5\text{ N}$$

Accounting for climb, maneuvering, and acceleration margins, average cruise
thrust typically operates at roughly *30 N to 40 N* (~3 to 4 kgf).
Step 2: Calculate Mass Fuel Consumption Rate

Small micro-turbojets (e.g., JetCat P100, KingTech K85) have a TSFC of
roughly *0.12 to 0.15 kg / (N =C2=B7 hr)* at cruise throttle settings.

Using a conservative TSFC of *$0.14\text{ kg/(N}\cdot\text{hr)}$* at an
average cruise thrust of *$35\text{ N}$*:
$$\text{Fuel Flow Rate } (\dot{m}) =3D T \times \text{TSFC}$$
$$\dot{m} =3D 35\text{ N} \times 0.14\text{ kg/(N}\cdot\text{hr)} =3D 4.9\t=
ext{
kg/hr}$$

Converting this to a per-minute rate:
$$\text{Per-minute consumption} =3D \frac{4.9\text{ kg/hr}}{60} \approx
\mathbf{0.0817\text{ kg/min}} \quad (\approx 81.7\text{ g/min})$$
Step 3: Calculate Required Usable Fuel Mass

For a 15-minute primary flight mission:
$$\text{Mission Fuel Mass} =3D 15\text{ min} \times 0.0817\text{ kg/min} =
=3D
\mathbf{1.225\text{ kg}}$$

Adding a standard *20% fuel reserve* (for taxi, climb, wind headroom, and
landing setup):
$$\text{Total Fuel Mass Required} =3D 1.225\text{ kg} \times 1.20 =3D
\mathbf{1.47\text{ kg}}$$
Step 4: Convert Mass to Tank Volume

Jet-A / Kerosene has a density of approximately *$0.80\text{ kg/L}$* at
standard temperature ($15^\circ\text{C}$).
$$\text{Total Tank Volume Required} =3D \frac{\text{Total Fuel
Mass}}{\text{Fuel Density}}$$
$$\text{Total Tank Volume Required} =3D \frac{1.47\text{ kg}}{0.80\text{
kg/L}} =3D \mathbf{1.8375\text{ Liters}} \approx \mathbf{1,840\text{ ml}}$$
